To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function from the `stdlib.h` library. Here's an example of how to use it:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your program logic here

    // To terminate the program, use the exit() function
    exit(0);
    return 0;
}
```

In the above code, the `exit(0)` function is called to terminate the program. The `0` argument passed to `exit()` is the exit status of the program. You can replace `0` with any other value to indicate a specific exit status if needed.

By calling `exit(0)`, the program will be terminated and the control will be returned to the operating system. The `return 0;` statement is optional and is typically used to indicate a successful program execution. However, in this case, the `exit()` function takes care of the program termination, so the `return 0;` statement can be omitted. 

Remember to include the necessary header file (`#include <stdlib.h>`) to use the `exit()` function. If you have any other code or logic in your program, you can place it before the `exit()` function call. The `exit()` function should be the last line of your `main()` function to ensure that the program terminates properly. 

By using the `exit()` function, you can terminate a C program when you want to end its execution. This can be useful in various scenarios, such as when a certain condition is met or when the user wants to exit the program. 

